WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.112
Here is a queue, and here is a stack

00:00:04.112 --> 00:00:05.348
both empty.

00:00:05.348 --> 00:00:09.573
We do the same seven operations on both

00:00:09.573 --> 00:00:13.488
insert 1, insert 2, insert 3

00:00:13.488 --> 00:00:17.300
remove, insert 4, remove, remove.

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:20.734
Which numbers does each structure give back

00:00:20.734 --> 00:00:21.435
in order?

00:00:21.435 --> 00:00:23.257
And what is left in each?

00:00:23.257 --> 00:00:25.570
Pause the video and work it out.

00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:31.727
Insert 1, 2 and 3.

00:00:31.727 --> 00:00:35.889
In the queue they line up from the front

00:00:35.889 --> 00:00:40.150
in the stack they pile up from the bottom.

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:41.181
Remove.

00:00:41.181 --> 00:00:43.711
The queue gives back 1

00:00:43.711 --> 00:00:45.658
the oldest element.

00:00:45.658 --> 00:00:49.551
The stack gives back 3, the newest.

00:00:49.551 --> 00:00:53.930
Insert 4: it joins the rear of the queue

00:00:53.930 --> 00:00:56.460
and the top of the stack.

00:00:56.810 --> 00:00:58.977
Remove: the queue gives 2

00:00:58.977 --> 00:01:00.680
the stack gives 4.

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:03.544
Remove again: the queue gives 3

00:01:03.544 --> 00:01:05.170
the stack gives 2.

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:09.374
So the queue gives back 1, 2, 3

00:01:09.374 --> 00:01:11.493
and still holds 4.

00:01:11.493 --> 00:01:15.251
The stack gives back 3, 4, 2

00:01:15.251 --> 00:01:17.274
and still holds 1.

00:01:17.274 --> 00:01:21.417
A queue always returns the oldest element

00:01:21.417 --> 00:01:23.440
a stack, the newest.

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:25.786
As an abstract data type

00:01:25.786 --> 00:01:27.552
a queue has two updates

00:01:27.552 --> 00:01:29.036
each at its own end.

00:01:29.036 --> 00:01:31.862
Enqueue inserts an element at the rear

00:01:31.862 --> 00:01:33.416
also called the tail.

00:01:33.416 --> 00:01:36.383
Dequeue removes the element at the front

00:01:36.383 --> 00:01:38.220
the head, and returns it.

00:01:38.570 --> 00:01:41.613
Together they give the rule first in

00:01:41.613 --> 00:01:45.314
first out, FIFO: the element that has waited

00:01:45.397 --> 00:01:47.124
longest leaves first.

00:01:47.124 --> 00:01:50.825
A stack inserts and removes at the same end

00:01:50.825 --> 00:01:53.210
so it is last in, first out.

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:56.096
A queue usually also has peek

00:01:56.180 --> 00:01:58.209
which looks at the front

00:01:58.294 --> 00:02:01.252
without removing it, is empty, and

00:02:01.252 --> 00:02:03.872
for a fixed capacity, is full.

00:02:03.957 --> 00:02:06.408
Dequeuing from an empty queue

00:02:06.493 --> 00:02:08.183
is called underflow

00:02:08.183 --> 00:02:11.480
enqueuing into a full one is overflow.

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:14.854
Queues are everywhere in computing

00:02:14.854 --> 00:02:17.727
buffers for music and network data

00:02:17.727 --> 00:02:20.201
processes waiting for the CPU

00:02:20.201 --> 00:02:23.314
and jobs waiting for a shared printer.

00:02:23.314 --> 00:02:25.548
Java has a Queue interface

00:02:25.548 --> 00:02:29.140
and ArrayDeque is the usual class behind it.

00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:34.306
Offer enqueues at the rear: report.pdf

00:02:34.306 --> 00:02:39.010
then photo.png, then slides.pptx.

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:43.174
Printing the queue lists it from the front

00:02:43.174 --> 00:02:45.214
report, photo, slides.

00:02:45.214 --> 00:02:49.205
Peek looks at the front without removing it

00:02:49.205 --> 00:02:51.600
next up is report.pdf.

00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:54.822
While the queue is not empty

00:02:54.913 --> 00:02:57.464
poll dequeues from the front

00:02:57.555 --> 00:02:59.650
and the job is printed.

00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:01.745
Report, photo, slides

00:03:01.745 --> 00:03:04.660
exactly the order they arrived.

00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:08.520
Poll on an empty queue does not crash

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:10.090
it returns null.

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:13.240
The Queue interface has two methods

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:14.840
for each operation.

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:17.720
Offer, poll and peek report failure

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:20.360
by returning false or null; add

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:22.360
remove and element throw

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:24.120
an exception instead.

00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:26.518
Now the implementations.

00:03:26.518 --> 00:03:29.926
A queue must reach both of its ends quickly.

00:03:29.926 --> 00:03:31.365
A linked list can

00:03:31.441 --> 00:03:33.410
the slides build it on the

00:03:33.486 --> 00:03:35.152
list of lecture seven

00:03:35.152 --> 00:03:37.121
with a dummy header node.

00:03:37.121 --> 00:03:39.469
Our Java version has no dummy.

00:03:39.469 --> 00:03:41.817
Head refers to the front node

00:03:41.817 --> 00:03:43.407
the next to dequeue

00:03:43.407 --> 00:03:45.452
and tail to the rear node

00:03:45.452 --> 00:03:47.573
the most recently enqueued.

00:03:47.573 --> 00:03:49.769
Here the queue holds only A

00:03:49.769 --> 00:03:52.420
so head and tail both point at it.

00:03:52.770 --> 00:03:55.296
A size counter makes isEmpty

00:03:55.383 --> 00:03:56.951
and size O(1).

00:03:56.951 --> 00:03:59.913
And isFull always returns false

00:03:59.913 --> 00:04:02.613
a linked list can always grow

00:04:02.613 --> 00:04:06.010
until the computer runs out of memory.

00:04:06.590 --> 00:04:07.501
Enqueue B.

00:04:07.501 --> 00:04:10.142
First, a new node holding B.

00:04:10.142 --> 00:04:12.327
The queue is not empty

00:04:12.418 --> 00:04:15.515
so tail.next = node links

00:04:15.606 --> 00:04:18.065
it behind the current rear.

00:04:18.065 --> 00:04:21.070
Then tail moves to the new node.

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:23.220
And size goes up by one.

00:04:23.570 --> 00:04:26.599
Enqueue C works the same way: a new node

00:04:26.599 --> 00:04:29.407
linked behind B, and tail moves to C.

00:04:29.407 --> 00:04:31.919
Only an empty queue is different

00:04:31.919 --> 00:04:35.170
then the new node becomes the head as well.

00:04:35.750 --> 00:04:38.619
Dequeue first checks for underflow

00:04:38.619 --> 00:04:42.062
on an empty queue it throws an exception.

00:04:42.062 --> 00:04:45.750
Otherwise it remembers the front element, A.

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:48.424
head = head.next

00:04:48.517 --> 00:04:50.656
unlinks the front node

00:04:50.656 --> 00:04:52.701
head now points at B.

00:04:52.701 --> 00:04:56.792
A is returned, and the old node is garbage.

00:04:56.792 --> 00:05:00.976
The queue is B, C, with peek B and size 2

00:05:00.976 --> 00:05:03.300
just as the demo prints.

00:05:03.650 --> 00:05:06.091
Why is the front at the head

00:05:06.091 --> 00:05:07.858
and not at the tail?

00:05:07.858 --> 00:05:10.888
Enqueue at the head would be cheap

00:05:10.972 --> 00:05:13.749
but dequeue at the tail must find

00:05:13.833 --> 00:05:15.937
the node before the tail

00:05:15.937 --> 00:05:18.041
to set its next to null.

00:05:18.041 --> 00:05:21.240
A singly linked list has no way back

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:24.690
so that is a walk from the head: O(n).

00:05:25.270 --> 00:05:26.408
One more case.

00:05:26.408 --> 00:05:29.822
Suppose the queue holds a single element

00:05:29.822 --> 00:05:31.935
7, and we dequeue it.

00:05:31.935 --> 00:05:35.430
head becomes head.next, which is null.

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:37.178
If we stopped there

00:05:37.178 --> 00:05:40.252
tail would still refer to the removed node

00:05:40.322 --> 00:05:43.606
and the next enqueue would link its node behind

00:05:43.676 --> 00:05:46.331
a node that is no longer in the queue

00:05:46.331 --> 00:05:48.008
while head stayed null.

00:05:48.078 --> 00:05:50.314
That is why dequeue sets tail to

00:05:50.384 --> 00:05:52.340
null when head becomes null.

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:54.715
The demo enqueues 7

00:05:54.715 --> 00:05:57.182
and the first dequeue prints it.

00:05:57.257 --> 00:06:00.248
The second dequeue finds the queue empty

00:06:00.323 --> 00:06:03.164
and throws an IllegalStateException

00:06:03.164 --> 00:06:06.080
underflow, so nothing more is printed.

00:06:06.430 --> 00:06:09.650
Every operation of the linked queue changes

00:06:09.725 --> 00:06:13.020
a constant number of references, so enqueue,

00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:16.390
dequeue, peek, and isEmpty are all O(1).

00:06:16.970 --> 00:06:18.013
Now an array.

00:06:18.013 --> 00:06:21.141
The elements sit in consecutive cells

00:06:21.141 --> 00:06:23.146
with two indices: front,

00:06:23.146 --> 00:06:25.713
the index of the front element

00:06:25.713 --> 00:06:27.959
starting at 0, and rear,

00:06:27.959 --> 00:06:30.445
the index of the rear element

00:06:30.445 --> 00:06:32.290
starting at -1.

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:35.402
Enqueue refuses, and returns false

00:06:35.402 --> 00:06:38.480
when rear is already at the last cell.

00:06:38.830 --> 00:06:42.495
Otherwise it increases size and rear

00:06:42.495 --> 00:06:45.565
and stores x at array[rear].

00:06:45.565 --> 00:06:49.428
Enqueue 10, 20, 30, and 40

00:06:49.428 --> 00:06:52.499
rear walks from 0 to 3.

00:06:52.499 --> 00:06:55.470
Now rear is at the last cell.

00:06:56.050 --> 00:06:59.024
dequeue takes array[front]

00:06:59.024 --> 00:07:03.228
then decreases size and increases front.

00:07:03.228 --> 00:07:07.638
dequeue gives 10, and front moves to 1.

00:07:07.638 --> 00:07:10.509
dequeue again gives 20

00:07:10.509 --> 00:07:12.970
and front moves to 2.

00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:16.131
Both operations are O(1).

00:07:16.131 --> 00:07:18.360
But watch the indices.

00:07:18.940 --> 00:07:21.756
The demo prints the full array

00:07:21.756 --> 00:07:23.845
then the two dequeues

00:07:23.845 --> 00:07:27.932
and the array with front 2 and rear 3.

00:07:27.932 --> 00:07:31.020
Then enqueue(50) returns false.

00:07:31.370 --> 00:07:33.169
The queue holds only two

00:07:33.244 --> 00:07:34.968
elements in four cells

00:07:34.968 --> 00:07:36.991
and still refuses a third

00:07:36.991 --> 00:07:39.165
cells 0 and 1 are free

00:07:39.165 --> 00:07:42.237
but front and rear only ever move right.

00:07:42.237 --> 00:07:45.460
Used forever, enqueue 1 and dequeue 1

00:07:45.460 --> 00:07:48.458
a queue walks off the end of any array

00:07:48.458 --> 00:07:49.582
however large.

00:07:49.582 --> 00:07:52.130
The array queue runs out of room.

00:07:52.480 --> 00:07:56.147
One way out is to shift: after each dequeue

00:07:56.147 --> 00:07:58.980
move every element one cell left

00:07:58.980 --> 00:08:00.980
so front stays at 0.

00:08:00.980 --> 00:08:01.980
That works

00:08:01.980 --> 00:08:05.480
but a dequeue now costs Θ(n) moves.

00:08:05.830 --> 00:08:08.086
The other way is to wrap around

00:08:08.086 --> 00:08:09.990
when rear reaches the end

00:08:09.990 --> 00:08:11.612
continue at cell 0

00:08:11.612 --> 00:08:13.798
which the dequeues have freed.

00:08:13.798 --> 00:08:15.772
That is the circular queue

00:08:15.772 --> 00:08:18.310
and every operation stays O(1).

00:08:18.890 --> 00:08:19.938
To sum up.

00:08:19.938 --> 00:08:23.501
One: a queue enqueues at the rear

00:08:23.605 --> 00:08:26.330
and dequeues at the front.

00:08:26.330 --> 00:08:28.530
First in, first out.

00:08:28.880 --> 00:08:31.168
Two: on a linked list with head

00:08:31.242 --> 00:08:32.717
and tail references

00:08:32.717 --> 00:08:35.300
every queue operation is O(1)

00:08:35.300 --> 00:08:37.440
and the queue is never full.

00:08:37.790 --> 00:08:41.426
Three: a plain array queue runs out of room

00:08:41.509 --> 00:08:43.740
because front and rear only

00:08:43.823 --> 00:08:45.310
move to the right.

00:08:45.660 --> 00:08:46.370
Try this.

00:08:46.449 --> 00:08:48.814
Why does the linked queue keep

00:08:48.893 --> 00:08:50.786
a tail reference at all?

00:08:50.786 --> 00:08:53.940
And what would enqueue cost without it?

00:08:54.290 --> 00:08:58.222
In part two, we bend the array into a ring

00:08:58.222 --> 00:09:00.050
the circular queue.

